Before doing anything, read and follow AGENTS.md and SECURITY_AUDIT.md.
If this prompt conflicts with those files, follow the stricter safety restriction.

You are performing a security audit of my own application, TixeNex.

You have:
1. A local clone of the exact commit currently deployed in production.
2. Permission to perform safe, low-volume security testing against:
   https://tixenex.com

Architecture:
- ASP.NET Core API
- Blazor WASM frontend
- SQL Server
- RabbitMQ
- MinIO
- Hangfire / background worker
- Docker Compose
- Caddy reverse proxy
- Cloudflare in front of the public domain

This is an AUDIT-ONLY pass.

Do not modify repository files yet.

Review both the source/deployment configuration and the running public application.

Focus particularly on:

- authentication
- authorization and role boundaries
- Demo User / Demo Agent / Demo Admin isolation
- IDOR / broken object-level authorization
- ticket visibility and assignment permissions
- upload/download authorization
- export authorization
- file path / filename handling
- JWT/cookie handling
- CORS
- CSRF where applicable
- XSS
- SQL injection
- SSRF
- open redirects
- information leakage
- security headers
- rate limiting
- abuse of demo session creation
- secrets accidentally committed to Git
- appsettings / environment configuration
- Docker security
- unnecessary exposed ports
- SQL Server configuration
- RabbitMQ configuration
- MinIO configuration
- Hangfire exposure/security
- dependency vulnerabilities
- production-only configuration mistakes
- startup/restart/healthcheck behavior

You may run tests locally in this disposable VM.

For https://tixenex.com:
- keep requests low-volume
- do not perform DoS/load testing
- do not brute-force credentials
- do not intentionally corrupt production data
- do not delete or modify existing production data
- perform destructive testing only against disposable data in the local audit environment
- do not attempt SSH access or privilege escalation on the VPS
- do not attempt to bypass Cloudflare by attacking infrastructure
- do not request production secrets

Do not report speculative vulnerabilities as confirmed.

For every finding include:

1. Severity: Critical / High / Medium / Low / Informational
2. Title
3. Finding state: Confirmed / Likely / Informational / Not reproducible
4. Exact affected file/code/configuration
5. Evidence
6. Realistic exploitation scenario
7. Reproduction steps where safe
8. Recommended remediation
9. Suggested test that proves the fix

At the end provide:

A. Critical/High deployment blockers
B. Medium findings
C. Low/hardening findings
D. Checks performed that appear safe
E. Recommended remediation order

Do not implement fixes until I explicitly approve the findings.